I'm afraid it isn't -- or at least, not one that is unique to English. I
trust you're aware that French, Spanish, Portuguese and Italian have all
originated from Latin; thus, we could say that Latin was a very flexible
language and it allowed such different ways of usage that it even became
several different languages. The same goes actually even for Finnish,
which has several dialects in Finland and also one in Sweden, called
Meänkieli. And yes, the grammar of Meänkieli is (as far as I know...)
quite different from the "actual" Finnish.

I think the main point here is not the flexibility of English compared to
other languages (1) but the fact that we're living in an age of
communication where English is available almost everywhere -- and on the
other hand, where it hasn't been available for a very long time. I think
it's much more difficult nowadays for a language to evolve into several
independent languages, or at least it's a bit too early to say anything
about that.

(1) A new train of thought that didn't quite fit in that paragraph: I
think it's not the languages that are flexible as such but the people who
are using the languages. It's not English tolerating the changes, it's
different people in different parts of the world making English more
"acceptable" to them -- that is, bringing parts of their own languages
into the mix. If you've studied any foreign languages, I'm sure you know
what I mean: in the beginning it's difficult not to use the grammar of
your own language, because that's the way you've learned to see the world.

Now, you gradually (and actually pretty quickly) learn about the grammar
of the language and get used to putting things in different ways for
different languages. That's especially easy if you're surrounded by the
foreign language you're trying to learn or hear it all the time from the
TV or radio or Internet or anything.

But let's say there's a country with not too many connections with the
outside world -- and by this I quite simply mean that there are no
satellite dishes in front of every apartment, so that "proper English" (be
it British or American) is not available to everyone -- but English is
still used as a common language. And let's also say there are a couple of
native languages used, also. As far as I know, the language would start to
evolve. Fast-forward a couple of decades or centuries, and you'd have a
language that an American probably wouldn't understand.

Flexibility? Or just the natural cycle of languages? I think the same
would go for just about any language.

And oh, there happened to be a short article about creoles in a Finnish
newspaper today. Seems like there are many creoles which have evolved from
French and Portuguese, so we'll have to dismiss the English creoles as a
proof for the flexibility of English.

To put it shortly: It's not that English is flexible, it's that English is
the language that is nowadays being used in (more or less) every part of
the world. It would be a miracle if it did not evolve into different
directions.

